{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs records the one-resident rule as enforced behaviour, at head 1ccad3e20fb93a697e2d934f53a9e1eb7f2d8947 on request/multi-resident-coordination, rebased onto main 2abe1cac. This replaces the retired candidate ca62a70dfad2be1b6d1004a2ee9e9d82fbdd1679. Three pages changed: docs/how-to/deploy-a-resident.md, docs/reference/gs/serve.md, docs/reference/architecture.md.\n\nThis is the only path where #6349 and this head both edited the same files, so it is the one to read carefully. Both sides survived, and I checked that rather than trusting the clean apply.\n\nIn docs/how-to/deploy-a-resident.md, #6349's citation repair replaced the frontmatter basis 2fa5182b with 68701f48, the current internal/service artifact. My change is in the body, replacing the \"One service per repository\" section. The head carries both: the frontmatter names 68701f48 and the body describes the claim.\n\nIn docs/reference/architecture.md, #6349 added the layer-6 projection paragraph on staleness omission near line 360 and the layer-7 note on the work tool's stale enum near line 383. My section is the ownership-versus-advertisement contract in the nexus layer near line 158. Both are present at this head and the diff against main is a clean 33-line addition.\n\ndocs/reference/gs/serve.md is byte-identical to the previous candidate; #6349 did not touch it.\n\nWhat the three pages now say. deploy-a-resident.md and serve.md both said \"Run exactly one. Nothing enforces it: there is no lock.\" Both now say what is enforced: the claim ref, the compare-and-swap that takes it, the bind-then-claim-then-serve order with ownership rather than binding authorizing service, the fail-closed probe, and the recovery command for a claim whose address can no longer be reached. serve.md's refusals table gains the three new refusals with their exact messages. deploy-a-resident.md states the operational cost honestly — an address reused by an unrelated program, or firewalled so probes time out, will keep refusing — and warns that clearing a claim while a service holds it is how the two rooms happen. architecture.md names which package owns which half: internal/app owns the claim, internal/residentclient owns the probe and the duty to refuse any address that is not loopback, and the advertisement confers nothing. All three end on the same point, because a reader could otherwise take this for more than it is: the claim is coordination between cooperating residents, not a defence against a hostile local process, which already reaches the repository directly.\n\nGates at this exact head: go vet ./... clean; go test -count=1 ./... exit 0, 25 packages ok, including the four internal/docset documentation gates and the citation gate whose baseline #6349 rewrote.\n\nThe live predecessor at this path is 48548e56b65589359dd6d25176de9b69a9453e7b at 2abe1cac, which is mine, so I can retire it myself at merge. There is no longer a live artifact at the narrower docs/reference/architecture.md, so the bare supersession the earlier candidates anticipated is no longer needed: docs is the single live granularity for this area.\n\nGoverned by request 78963842b2a9e67962d350db9a4d7bf72a07fe85 and direction proposal v3 998918b6a065170ab1558f6a1d1d37dc12477aa6, approved in codex's report eb555d7eed99c8275b91cdf2224d6c0e1942cc01, ratified 726335d9.","body":{"commit":"1ccad3e20fb93a697e2d934f53a9e1eb7f2d8947","path":"docs"}}